been taken into consideration for safety evaluation have been related to the vehicle control; for example, deviations from vehicle trajectories deriving from the system use [5]. Other parameters include drivers’ visual strategies, visual demand deriving from the use of on-board screens [6], general driver behaviours [7, 8] and overall driver workload according to the situation [9].
The level of driver’s workload is linked to the difﬁculty of the task he experienced, and to his choice of strategies between the effort and performance trade-off [10]. Indeed, the individual can adapt his behaviour facing an increased task complexity with two possibilities: a higher investment of effort with no perceptible effect on performance; or, on the contrary, no increase in effort leading to a lower level of performance. Then, moderate increases in task difﬁculty may produce few observable changes in error rate, as the driver attempts to keep performance constant by allocating more resources to the task [11]. Furthermore, interindividual strategies are variables; some individuals develop more effective strategies which require less effort to reach a level of performance than do others. Hence, for all these reasons, objective performance measures, which directly measure performance, are not sufﬁcient by themselves to evaluate the overall demands of a given situation, data on effort have also to be taken into account.
Mental workload is a psychological construct, difﬁcult to deﬁne and difﬁcult to assess. O’Donnell & Eggemeir [12] deﬁned workload as being this portion of the operator’s limited capacity that is actually required to perform a particular task. According to this deﬁnition, mental workload depends upon the task demands in relation to the amount of resources the operator is willing or able to allocate, and is therefore a relative concept [13]. In order to deﬁne workload, the concept of ‘effort’ is of a primary importance: processing effort in resource allocation and effort for mobilisation of additional resources as a compensatory process, in relation to task demand [14, 15]. Although there is no universally accepted deﬁnition of mental workload, a consensus suggests that mental workload can be conceptualised as the interaction between the structure of systems and tasks on the one hand, and the capabilities, motivation and state of the human operator on the other [16 – 18].
Several methods have been developed to measure mental workload [19]: measurements of physiological parameters, such as heart rate [20], dual-task method [21]; and methods that elicit drivers’ subjective judgments about the workload they have experienced. The latter include SWAT (subjective workload assessment technique; [22] and the NASA-TLX – Task Load Index [23]). Subjective measures allow estimates from individual’s reports concerning the workload or effort expenditure that was experienced during task [24]. These measures are often used in practice because they have many practical advantages over objective measures [25 – 27].

The next section of this paper discusses the subjective methods for the evaluation of drivers’ mental workload, and, more speciﬁcally, on a revised version of the NASA-TLX.
2 The DALI as a method for subjective evaluation of mental workload
The subjective method allow for comparative assessment of mental workload in different situations. It can therefore be regarded as a relative measure. Subjective evaluation is often conducted in association with other workload measurement techniques [28].
The SWAT is a sophisticated workload assessment tool, composed of a two-step process: in a scale development phase, data necessary to develop a workload scale are obtained from individuals; during an event scoring phase, individuals rate the workload associated with a particular task [29]. The primary assumption of SWAT is that workload is a function of three dimensions: time load, mental effort load and psychological stress, each dimension having three possible levels. All possible combinations of the three levels of each dimension yield a 27-cell, threedimensional, matrix to represent workload.
The NASA-TLX method assumes that workload is inﬂuenced by mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, frustration level and effort. After assessing the magnitude of each of these six factors on a scale, the individual performs pairwise comparisons between these six factors, in order to determine the higher source of workload factor for each pair. A composite note quantifying the level of workload is set up by using both factor rating and relative weights computed from the comparison phase.
The NASA-TLX has been tested and used by the army and has been considered superior in terms of sensitivity than other methods and well accepted by the operator [30].
The DALI (Driving Activity Load Index) is a revised version of the NASA-TLX, adapted to the driving task. As previously mentioned, mental workload is multidimensional and, among other things, depends upon the type of loading task. The NASA-TLX was originally designed to assess pilot workload in the aviation domain. The goal was to develop a test better able to assess driving workload.
In this paper, the intention is not to develop principles and modes of scoring of the NASA-TLX, numerous literatures since 1988 have covered this topic. It should be stressed that, except for the deﬁnition of the main factors, the basic principles of DALI are the same as those for the TLX. In both tools, a scale rating procedure is deﬁned for six pre-deﬁned factors, followed by a weighting procedure in order to combine the six individual scales into a global

