3.1 Study design
As the objective of the experiment was to test the method, the driving conditions have been set up to induce on purpose various levels of workload for the driver. Indeed, various complexities of the context will allow to evaluate if the tool reﬂects correctly what is expected according to the driving conditions, and in which way the data resulting from the subjective evaluation correspond to the level of workload deliberately induced on the driver.
The four experimental sessions, of 15 min each, conducted on real road presented the following characteristics:
† to vary according to the level of workload induced on the driver;
† to be as realistic as possible in a context of the driving task.
Two situations with a high-task demand: High
(context þ system) HCS: While driving, the driver had to run a task according to stimulations emitted by an onboard system. The information to deal with is not directly linked to the driving task and induced a manual action or a verbal answer for the driver. The route to follow is given by a guidance system. The workload was linked to perceptual processes, decision making and motor and/or verbal output.
High (context) HC: Before the experimentation started, the driver had to consult a paper map to learn about and to memorise the route to follow. Then, he can stop anytime to check again the directions. The workload was linked to the mental representation of the route and to memorise it.
Two situations with a low-task demand: Low
(context þ system) LCS: The driver had to follow the route according to visual and auditory information given by a guidance system. The workload was linked to perceptual processes but the decision making, and the mental representation/memorisation was lighter than in the previous sessions.
Low (context) LC: During the route, the experimenter gave on time and clear directions to be followed by the driver. The workload was linked only with the management of the driving task, without any added activity.
In order to evaluate the efﬁciency of the DALI as a tool for the assessment of mobile phone use, an experiment was carried out in a real-road context (see [31] for detail of the experimental protocol and results). The objective was to investigate the evaluation of perceptual and cognitive load for the driver in this phoning condition, knowing that speciﬁc factors of the DALI were dedicated to these aspects. Results indicated that the global value of mental load increased signiﬁcantly when phoning and driving in comparison with the reference situation

corresponding to no system use. The load index was signiﬁcantly high for ‘auditory’ and ‘interference’ factors, in addition to ‘stress’. The effort of ‘attention’, although higher than during a simple driving task, does not increase signiﬁcantly. Hence, in terms of subjective evaluation of the workload, drivers identiﬁed the disturbance induced by phoning, through the perceptive channel of audition, and through the management of the driving task which induced stress. By this example, it is possible to illustrate how this tool based upon subjective evaluation and driver awareness can allow for the understanding of mental workload and cost of the task.
Hence, these four situations were identiﬁed, from high to low demand: ‘complex system’ requiring cognitive and perceptive attentional demand, ‘paper map’ with no system, ‘guidance system’ correctly designed and ‘human co-pilot’ giving instructions to the driver.
3.2 Equipment
The same instrumented vehicle has been used for the four sessions. For the complex session HCS, a speciﬁc software has been developed by our laboratory in order to display various types of auditory and visual information on a system that has been implemented on the dashboard. For the LCS session, a classical navigation system GPS has been implemented on the dashboard. This type of system displayed clear and simple auditory messages (e.g. ‘be prepared to turn left’ then ‘turn left’), in addition to guidance visual displays (directional arrows or schema of roundabouts) concerning driving manoeuvres during the route. The use of this system did not require any speciﬁc training.
3.3 Subjects and procedure
A group of ten experienced middle-aged drivers have been driving in urban and peri-urban areas. After a period of 15 min of familiarisation with the vehicle, each of them had to go through the four sessions described above, the sequence order of these sessions being randomly organised. The subjective evaluation tools have been applied at the end of each of these four sessions.
To summarise, the process for the experimental procedure was as follows:
† To set up diversiﬁed situations varying on purpose by their level of demand: cognitive process (e.g. to memorise the route) and perceptivo-motor process (e.g. to run manual action following auditory, visual or tactile stimulations).
† To apply the tool for each of these sessions in order to gather subjective data.
† To check that the highly demand session corresponds to the higher values for the tools and to identify in which way.

